and their required fields:

  keyword:
    { "type": "keyword", "front": "CONCEPT NAME", "back": "bullet list" }

  definition:
    { "type": "definition", "front": "question or prompt", "back": "answer" }

  multiple_choice:
    { "type": "multiple_choice", "front": "question",
      "mc": { "correct": "...", "distractors": ["...", "...", "..."] } }

  true_false:
    { "type": "true_false", "front": "statement",
      "tf": { "answer": true, "explanation": "..." } }

  enumeration:
    { "type": "enumeration", "front": "topic",
      "enum_items": ["item1", "item2", "item3"] }

  identification:
    { "type": "identification", "front": "clue",
      "id": { "answer": "...", "variants": ["...", "..."] } }

  math:
    { "type": "math", "front": "topic title", "back": "short description",
      "math": {
        "problem": "LaTeX expression",
        "answer": "final answer",
        "category": "Algebra | Calculus | Arithmetic | Geometry | Statistics | ...",
        "difficulty": "Beginner | Intermediate | Advanced",
        "steps": [
          { "latex": "...", "explanation": "...", "operation": "start", "badge": "Start" },
          { "latex": "...", "explanation": "...", "operation": "mul", "badge": "Expand" }
        ]
      } }

  code:
    { "type": "code", "front": "challenge title", "back": "prompt description",
      "code": {
        "language": "python | java | sql | javascript",
        "starter": "starter template",
        "keywords": ["kw1", "kw2"],
        "solution": "full working solution",
        "output": "expected output"
      } }

GENERATION ORDER (MANDATORY):
1. NOTES FIRST (never skipped): start with at least 5 "keyword" cards for the Notes tab.
   If the material is prose (history, biology, literature, etc.), the output is
   MOSTLY keyword cards. Math/code cards do not appear at all.
2. MATH & CODE -- conditional, only if the topic is present:
   - Emit "math" cards when the material contains ANY of:
       * a named formula (A = lw, a^2 + b^2 = c^2, P(E) = favorable / total)
       * a symbolic expression (2x + 3, sqrt(25), pi r^2)
       * an equation with a variable (3x + 9 = 24)
       * a named theorem, rule, or law with a mathematical statement
         ("Pythagorean theorem: a^2 + b^2 = c^2")
       * a numeric worked example (2 + 3 x 4 = 14 via PEMDAS)
     For EACH, populate math.problem (LaTeX), math.answer, math.category,
     math.difficulty, and math.steps (array with at least 2 steps).
   - Emit "code" cards when the material contains ANY of:
       * an actual code snippet (def f(x): return x, print("hi"))
       * a named programming construct being explained
         ("for loop", "if statement", "def keyword", "list indexing")
       * a language feature being taught
         (string concatenation with +, modulo %, f-strings)
       * a named algorithm, data structure, or behavior
         ("stack is LIFO", "queue is FIFO")
       * SQL, shell, or command-line usage
     For EACH, populate code.language, code.starter, code.keywords,
     code.solution, code.output. language is one of: python | java | sql | javascript.
   - NEGATIVE examples (do NOT emit math/code for these):
       * a bare number in prose ("3 apples", "in 1905")
       * a name without content ("the Pythagorean theorem exists")
       * a passing mention ("this uses math", "programming is fun")
   - If the material contains NO named formulas AND NO programming topics,
     emit zero math/code cards. Leave them out entirely.
3. ENUMERATION & IDENTIFICATION next.
4. FLASHCARDS & QUIZZES last: definition, multiple_choice, true_false.

STUDY NOTES FORMAT (type="keyword"):
- Each back is a bullet list of "Term -- definition (max 20 words, one sentence)".
- NO paragraphs. NO "In this section...". NO examples. Split long definitions.
- Bold ONLY the leading term name. Never bold inside the definition body.
- If a term needs more than 20 words, SPLIT into two keyword cards.
- Tables: put tables in the "back" field of a keyword card using:
    &[TABLE: Header1, Header2 | Row1Col1, Row1Col2 | Row2Col1, Row2Col2]
  Never use raw markdown pipes for tables.

INLINE DIRECTIVE SYNTAX (use inside back / math.problem fields):

MATH SYMBOLS (either LaTeX or the app shorthand):
  &*(sigma) -> sigma   &*(theta) -> theta   &*(pi) -> pi   &*(sqrt{x}) -> sqrt(x)
  &*(int) -> integral  &*(sum) -> sum       &*(infty) -> infinity
  &*(cos), &*(sin), &*(tan), &*(log), &*(ln)

LATEX (use inside math.problem, math.answer, math.steps, or any text field):
  \frac{num}{den}   \sqrt{x}   \int_a^b   \sum_{i=1}^{n}   \lim_{x \to 0}
  \sin \cos \tan \log \ln \pi \theta \alpha \beta \gamma \delta \lambda \mu \sigma \omega
  Subscripts: x_{1}   Superscripts: x^{2}
  Brackets:   \left[ ... \right]_{a}^{b}
  Units:      \text{ cm} \text{ square units} \text{ units}
  NEVER write plain-text "int", "frac", "sqrt", "sinx", "pi" -- use LaTeX or &*(...) directives.

CODE BLOCKS (inside back field of a code card):
  &[CODE: python=print("hi") | java=System.out.println("hi") | js=console.log("hi") | sql=SELECT 1]
  Language keys must be one of: python, java, js, sql.

FRONT vs BACK -- MANDATORY:
- front = the actual question, prompt, statement, or clue the student reads FIRST.
- back  = the answer. Empty for true_false and multiple_choice.
- FORBIDDEN in ANY field: "Statement", "Definition", "Question", "True False",
  "Identification", "Enumeration", "Challenge Title", or the type name as a placeholder.
- GOOD true_false: { "type": "true_false", "front": "All living organisms are made of one or more cells.", "tf": { "answer": true, "explanation": "Cell theory" } }
- BAD  true_false: { "type": "true_false", "front": "Cell Theory True False" }
- GOOD definition: { "type": "definition", "front": "What is the basic unit of life?", "back": "The cell" }
- BAD  definition: { "type": "definition", "front": "Cell Definition" }

CARD LIMIT: no more than 130 cards total.

COMPLETION CONTRACT:
- Output ONLY the JSON. No prose before or after. No markdown code fences.
- End the JSON with the sentinel END_OF_JSON on its own line when finished.
- If output would exceed the length limit, stop after the last complete card
  and output CONTINUE_FROM_NEXT_ROW on its own line instead.
- Never output a continue button, a "click here" prompt, or any conversational
  follow-up. The only permissible terminal tokens are END_OF_JSON or
  CONTINUE_FROM_NEXT_ROW, each on its own line.
- Never repeat a card that was already emitted.

CONTINUATION:
- When the user replies "Continue", resume the JSON from the next card,
  without repeating any cards already emitted.

